\description{
Recomputes the doubly robust average treatment effect (ATE) within each
level of the chosen categorical variables from a \code{\link[=get_hte]{get_hte()}} result and
draws them with \pkg{forestplot}, in the diamond style of
\code{RegR::plt_eff2()}, wrapped as a ggplot by \code{\link[ggplotify:as.ggplot]{ggplotify::as.ggplot()}} so
\pkg{patchwork} can combine it with other plots. The estimates come from the
stored forest exactly as \code{get_hte(sub_var = )} computes them, so nothing is
refitted and the subgroups can be chosen after the fact.
}
\section{Subgroup estimates}{

Every row is the doubly robust estimate within the subgroup, as in
\code{get_hte()$subgroup}: \code{\link[grf:average_treatment_effect]{grf::average_treatment_effect()}} with \code{subset} for
\code{"diff"}, and the arm scores averaged over the subgroup for \code{"ratio"} and
\code{"OR"}. The counts are patients per arm, treated first. A level with fewer
than two patients in either arm -- or, for a survival outcome, no patient
in an arm followed beyond \code{time} -- has no estimate: it is drawn with a
dash, with a warning.
}

\section{Replaced rows}{

A row named in \code{effect} is drawn with the supplied estimate and interval,
taken as they are: at \code{conf_level}, as a difference for \code{"diff"} and a
ratio for \code{"ratio"} and \code{"OR"}. Its standard error and \code{P} are backed out
of the interval with the normal approximation,
\eqn{SE = (hi - lo) / (2 z)} with \eqn{z} the normal quantile of
\code{conf_level}, on the log scale for a ratio. A level without an estimate
can be filled in this way. The "P for interaction" of its variable and its
\code{cate_mean} keep the values from the forest: the Wald test cannot be redone
from a replaced row.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("grf", quietly = TRUE) && requireNamespace("forestplot", quietly = TRUE) && requireNamespace("ggplotify", quietly = TRUE) && requireNamespace("patchwork", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
\donttest{
set.seed(20260923)
n <- 600
d <- data.frame(age   = round(runif(n, 20, 85)),
                sex   = factor(sample(c("F", "M"), n, replace = TRUE)),
                stage = factor(sample(c("I", "II", "III"), n, replace = TRUE)))
d$z <- rbinom(n, 1, 0.5)
d$y <- rbinom(n, 1, plogis(-1 + 0.02 * (d$age - 50) +
                             d$z * (0.2 + 0.6 * (d$sex == "M"))))
res <- get_hte(d, cat_var = "z", adj_var = c("age", "sex", "stage"),
               surv = "y", grf_args = list(num.trees = 500, seed = 1))

# Every categorical covariate, the overall ATE on top
plt_hte_sub(res)

# Chosen variables as risk ratios, with both P columns
plt_hte_sub(res, sub_var = c("sex", "stage"), measure = "ratio",
            show_pvalue = TRUE, show_pinter = TRUE)

# A ggplot, so patchwork stacks it over the CATE density by sex
patchwork::wrap_plots(plt_hte_sub(res, sub_var = "sex"),
                      plt_hte_cate(res, sub_var = "sex", type = "density"),
                      ncol = 1)

# Replace rows, e.g. with published estimates: drawn as given, with the
# P backed out of each interval
plt_hte_sub(res, sub_var = c("sex", "stage"), show_pvalue = TRUE,
            effect = list(sex = c(M = "0.150 (0.050, 0.250)"),
                          "All patients" = "0.100 (0.040, 0.160)"))
}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
